          "answer": "Based on reading `src/shipping/carriers.py`, here's what it does:\n\n**Carrier selection and rate quoting:**\n\n- **`CARRIERS`** dict defines three shipping options with their pricing:\n  - *ravenpost*: $4.20 base + $0.85/kg\n  - *tortoise*: $2.10 base + $1.60/kg  \n  - *kestrel*: $6.00 base + $0.40/kg\n\n- **`_failures`** tracks failure counts per carrier (starts at 0 for each)\n\n**Functions:**\n\n- **`quote(name, weight_kg)`** \u2014 Returns the cost to carrier `name` for a package of `weight_kg` kg: `round(base + per_kg * weight_kg, 2)`\n\n- **`cheapest(weight_kg)`** \u2014 Filters out carriers that have already had 3+ failures (`_failures[n] < 3`), then returns the carrier with the lowest quote for the given weight\n\n- **`record_failure(name)`** \u2014 Increments the failure count for a carrier and returns the new count. After three consecutive failures, that carrier is automatically excluded from `cheapest()` selections.\n\n**Design intent:** The system silently switches to the next cheapest available carrier when one starts failing, without raising errors. This connects to the runbook note: \"Three failures in a row and the carrier is skipped for the rest of the run.\"",
